    "marksCategory": "All",
    "title": "Graph Introduction & Terminologies",
    "content": "# Graph Introduction & Terminologies — Data Structures\n\n> **Definition:** A **Graph** is a non-linear data structure denoted as **G = (V, E)**, consisting of a non-empty finite set of **Vertices (Nodes) V** and a set of **Edges (Arcs) E** connecting pairs of vertices.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Detailed Technical Explanation\n\n```\nUNDIRECTED GRAPH:                     DIRECTED GRAPH (DIGRAPH):\n     ( 1 ) -------- ( 2 )                  ( 1 ) ------> ( 2 )\n       |     \\        |                      |    \\        |\n       |       \\      |                      |      \\      v\n       |         \\    |                      v        v   ( 4 )\n     ( 3 ) -------- ( 4 )                  ( 3 ) <------ ( 4 )\n```\n\n### Core Graph Terminologies:\n1. **Vertex (Node):** An individual data point or entity in the graph.\n2. **Edge (Arc):** A link or line connecting two vertices **(u, v)**.\n3. **Directed Graph (Digraph):** A graph where edges have a defined direction (ordered pair $(u, v) \ne (v, u)$).\n4. **Undirected Graph:** A graph where edges are bidirectional and symmetric (**(u, v) = (v, u)**).\n5. **Weighted Graph:** A graph where each edge is assigned a numerical cost, distance, or weight **w(u, v)**.\n6. **Degree of a Vertex:**\n   - **In Undirected Graphs:** The total number of edges connected to that vertex.\n   - **In Directed Graphs:**\n     - **In-Degree:** Number of edges directed *into* the vertex.\n     - **Out-Degree:** Number of edges directed *out of* the vertex.\n7. **Handshaking Lemma:** In any undirected graph, the sum of degrees of all vertices equals twice the number of edges:\n   ```\n   Sum of Deg(v) = 2 * |E|\n   ```\n8. **Adjacent Vertices (Neighbors):** Two vertices connected directly by an edge.\n9. **Path:** A sequence of alternating vertices and edges connecting a source vertex to a destination vertex.\n10. **Cycle:** A closed path where the start vertex and end vertex are the same, with no edge repeated.\n11. **Acyclic Graph:** A graph containing zero cycles (e.g., Trees, Directed Acyclic Graphs - DAG).\n12. **Connected Graph (Undirected):** A graph where there exists at least one path between every pair of vertices.\n13. **Strongly Connected Graph (Directed):** A digraph where there is a directed path from every vertex to every other vertex.\n14. **Complete Graph (Kn):** A graph where every vertex is connected to every other vertex. Number of edges in **K_n**:\n    ```\n    |E| = n * (n - 1) / 2\n    ```\n\n---\n\n## 2. Memory Keywords & Properties\n- **Vertices & Edges: G = (V, E)**.\n- **Handshaking Lemma: Sum deg(v) = 2|E|** (Total odd degree vertices is always EVEN).\n- **Dense vs Sparse Graph:** Dense when **|E| pprox |V|^2**; Sparse when **|E| pprox |V|**.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Must-Write Points for Exams\n- In an undirected complete graph with **N** vertices, the maximum number of edges is **N(N - 1) / 2**.\n- In a directed complete graph with **N** vertices, the maximum number of edges is **N(N - 1)**.\n- Trees are a special type of connected, acyclic undirected graph with **N - 1** edges.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Quick Recall Flow\n```\nG = (V, E) -> Directed vs Undirected -> In/Out Degree -> Handshaking Lemma (Sum Deg = 2E) -> Complete Graph E = N(N-1)/2\n```\n",
    "html": "<h1>Graph Introduction &amp; Terminologies — Data Structures</h1>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Definition:</strong> A <strong>Graph</strong> is a non-linear data structure denoted as <strong>G = (V, E)</strong>, consisting of a non-empty finite set of <strong>Vertices (Nodes) V</strong> and a set of <strong>Edges (Arcs) E</strong> connecting pairs of vertices.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr />\n<h2>1. Detailed Technical Explanation</h2>\n<pre><code>UNDIRECTED GRAPH:                     DIRECTED GRAPH (DIGRAPH):\n     ( 1 ) -------- ( 2 )                  ( 1 ) ------&gt; ( 2 )\n       |     \\        |                      |    \\        |\n       |       \\      |                      |      \\      v\n       |         \\    |                      v        v   ( 4 )\n     ( 3 ) -------- ( 4 )                  ( 3 ) &lt;------ ( 4 )\n</code></pre>\n<h3>Core Graph Terminologies:</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Vertex (Node):</strong> An individual data point or entity in the graph.</li>\n<li><strong>Edge (Arc):</strong> A link or line connecting two vertices <strong>(u, v)</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Directed Graph (Digraph):</strong> A graph where edges have a defined direction (ordered pair $(u, v) <br />\ne (v, u)$).</li>\n<li><strong>Undirected Graph:</strong> A graph where edges are bidirectional and symmetric (<strong>(u, v) = (v, u)</strong>).</li>\n<li><strong>Weighted Graph:</strong> A graph where each edge is assigned a numerical cost, distance, or weight <strong>w(u, v)</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Degree of a Vertex:</strong><br />\n   - <strong>In Undirected Graphs:</strong> The total number of edges connected to that vertex.<br />\n   - <strong>In Directed Graphs:</strong><ul>\n<li><strong>In-Degree:</strong> Number of edges directed <em>into</em> the vertex.</li>\n<li><strong>Out-Degree:</strong> Number of edges directed <em>out of</em> the vertex.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Handshaking Lemma:</strong> In any undirected graph, the sum of degrees of all vertices equals twice the number of edges:<br />\n<code>Sum of Deg(v) = 2 * |E|</code></li>\n<li><strong>Adjacent Vertices (Neighbors):</strong> Two vertices connected directly by an edge.</li>\n<li><strong>Path:</strong> A sequence of alternating vertices and edges connecting a source vertex to a destination vertex.</li>\n<li><strong>Cycle:</strong> A closed path where the start vertex and end vertex are the same, with no edge repeated.</li>\n<li><strong>Acyclic Graph:</strong> A graph containing zero cycles (e.g., Trees, Directed Acyclic Graphs - DAG).</li>\n<li><strong>Connected Graph (Undirected):</strong> A graph where there exists at least one path between every pair of vertices.</li>\n<li><strong>Strongly Connected Graph (Directed):</strong> A digraph where there is a directed path from every vertex to every other vertex.</li>\n<li><strong>Complete Graph (Kn):</strong> A graph where every vertex is connected to every other vertex. Number of edges in <strong>K_n</strong>:<br />\n<code>|E| = n * (n - 1) / 2</code></li>\n</ol>\n<hr />\n<h2>2. Memory Keywords &amp; Properties</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Vertices &amp; Edges: G = (V, E)</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Handshaking Lemma: Sum deg(v) = 2|E|</strong> (Total odd degree vertices is always EVEN).</li>\n<li><strong>Dense vs Sparse Graph:</strong> Dense when <strong>|E| pprox |V|^2</strong>; Sparse when <strong>|E| pprox |V|</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h2>3. Must-Write Points for Exams</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>In an undirected complete graph with <strong>N</strong> vertices, the maximum number of edges is <strong>N(N - 1) / 2</strong>.</li>\n<li>In a directed complete graph with <strong>N</strong> vertices, the maximum number of edges is <strong>N(N - 1)</strong>.</li>\n<li>Trees are a special type of connected, acyclic undirected graph with <strong>N - 1</strong> edges.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h2>4. Quick Recall Flow</h2>\n<pre><code>G = (V, E) -&gt; Directed vs Undirected -&gt; In/Out Degree -&gt; Handshaking Lemma (Sum Deg = 2E) -&gt; Complete Graph E = N(N-1)/2\n</code></pre>",
