    "marksCategory": "All",
    "title": "CPU Performance Measures",
    "content": "﻿# Topic: Performance Measures of Computer Architecture\n\n**Q. How is the performance of a computer system measured? Discuss CPU Execution Time, Clock Rate, and the various factors that affect computer performance.**\n\n---\n\n> 📌 **Definition to Remember**\n> Computer **Performance** measures how quickly a system executes tasks. It is inversely proportional to execution time (Performance = 1 / (Execution Time)). The primary metric is **CPU Execution Time**, which depends on Instruction Count, CPI (Cycles Per Instruction), and Clock Rate.\n\n---\n\n# CPU Performance Measures\nThe actual time the CPU spends computing a specific task (excludes I/O wait time).\n**Formulas:**\n* CPU Execution Time = CPU Clock Cycles × Clock Cycle Time\n* CPU Execution Time = CPU Clock Cycles / (Clock Rate)\n\n### 2. Clock Rate and Clock Cycle\n* **Clock Cycle (Period):** The fundamental unit of time in a computer (e.g., 1  ns), generated by a hardware oscillator.\n* **Clock Rate (Frequency):** The inverse of cycle time. Number of cycles per second, measured in **Hertz (Hz)** (e.g., 3  GHz). Higher clock rate generally means faster execution.\n\n### 3. Other Key Performance Metrics\n* **CPI (Cycles Per Instruction):** Average number of clock cycles needed to execute one instruction.\n  * Execution Time = Instruction Count × CPI × Clock Cycle Time\n* **MIPS (Millions of Instructions Per Second):** A metric expressing execution speed.\n  * MIPS = Instruction Count / (Execution Time × 10^6)\n\n### 4. Factors Affecting Computer Performance\n\n| Factor | Impact on Performance |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| **Clock Speed** | Higher clock speed executes instructions in less time. |\n| **Compiler Efficiency** | Better compilers generate fewer machine instructions (lowers Instruction Count). |\n| **Architecture / CPI** | Pipelining, superscalar, and multi-core designs lower the average CPI. |\n| **Cache Memory** | Fast cache reduces time the CPU spends waiting for data from main memory. |\n| **Bus Width** | A wider data bus transfers more data per cycle, reducing memory bottleneck. |\n\n---\n\n> ⭐ **Must-Write Points (for 10 marks)**\n> 1. Performance is inversely proportional to execution time: Perf = 1 / Time.\n> 2. **CPU Execution Time** = (Instruction Count) × (CPI) × (Clock Cycle Time).\n> 3. **Clock Cycle**: Fundamental time unit; **Clock Rate**: Cycles per second (Hz).\n> 4. **CPI**: Cycles Per Instruction. Lower CPI means better architecture (via pipelining).\n> 5. **MIPS**: Millions of Instructions Per Second (measures execution speed rate).\n> 6. Compiler efficiency affects the total Instruction Count.\n> 7. Cache memory and Bus Width heavily impact performance by reducing data fetch delays.\n\n---\n\n> ⚡ **Quick Recall**\n> `Performance = 1/Execution Time → Exec Time = (Instruction Count × CPI) / Clock Rate → CPI = Cycles per Instruction → Factors: Clock Speed, Compiler, Pipelining (CPI), Cache, Bus Width`\n\n",
    "html": "<p>﻿# Topic: Performance Measures of Computer Architecture</p>\n<p><strong>Q. How is the performance of a computer system measured? Discuss CPU Execution Time, Clock Rate, and the various factors that affect computer performance.</strong></p>\n<hr />\n<blockquote>\n<p>📌 <strong>Definition to Remember</strong><br />\nComputer <strong>Performance</strong> measures how quickly a system executes tasks. It is inversely proportional to execution time (Performance = 1 / (Execution Time)). The primary metric is <strong>CPU Execution Time</strong>, which depends on Instruction Count, CPI (Cycles Per Instruction), and Clock Rate.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr />\n<h1>CPU Performance Measures</h1>\n<p>The actual time the CPU spends computing a specific task (excludes I/O wait time).<br />\n<strong>Formulas:</strong><br />\n* CPU Execution Time = CPU Clock Cycles × Clock Cycle Time<br />\n* CPU Execution Time = CPU Clock Cycles / (Clock Rate)</p>\n<h3>2. Clock Rate and Clock Cycle</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Clock Cycle (Period):</strong> The fundamental unit of time in a computer (e.g., 1  ns), generated by a hardware oscillator.</li>\n<li><strong>Clock Rate (Frequency):</strong> The inverse of cycle time. Number of cycles per second, measured in <strong>Hertz (Hz)</strong> (e.g., 3  GHz). Higher clock rate generally means faster execution.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>3. Other Key Performance Metrics</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>CPI (Cycles Per Instruction):</strong> Average number of clock cycles needed to execute one instruction.</li>\n<li>Execution Time = Instruction Count × CPI × Clock Cycle Time</li>\n<li><strong>MIPS (Millions of Instructions Per Second):</strong> A metric expressing execution speed.</li>\n<li>MIPS = Instruction Count / (Execution Time × 10^6)</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>4. Factors Affecting Computer Performance</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Factor</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Impact on Performance</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Clock Speed</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Higher clock speed executes instructions in less time.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Compiler Efficiency</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Better compilers generate fewer machine instructions (lowers Instruction Count).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Architecture / CPI</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Pipelining, superscalar, and multi-core designs lower the average CPI.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Cache Memory</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Fast cache reduces time the CPU spends waiting for data from main memory.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Bus Width</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">A wider data bus transfers more data per cycle, reducing memory bottleneck.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<hr />\n<blockquote>\n<p>⭐ <strong>Must-Write Points (for 10 marks)</strong><br />\n1. Performance is inversely proportional to execution time: Perf = 1 / Time.<br />\n2. <strong>CPU Execution Time</strong> = (Instruction Count) × (CPI) × (Clock Cycle Time).<br />\n3. <strong>Clock Cycle</strong>: Fundamental time unit; <strong>Clock Rate</strong>: Cycles per second (Hz).<br />\n4. <strong>CPI</strong>: Cycles Per Instruction. Lower CPI means better architecture (via pipelining).<br />\n5. <strong>MIPS</strong>: Millions of Instructions Per Second (measures execution speed rate).<br />\n6. Compiler efficiency affects the total Instruction Count.<br />\n7. Cache memory and Bus Width heavily impact performance by reducing data fetch delays.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr />\n<blockquote>\n<p>⚡ <strong>Quick Recall</strong><br />\n<code>Performance = 1/Execution Time → Exec Time = (Instruction Count × CPI) / Clock Rate → CPI = Cycles per Instruction → Factors: Clock Speed, Compiler, Pipelining (CPI), Cache, Bus Width</code></p>\n</blockquote>",
