    "title": "Basic Organization of Computer (Self-Learning)",
    "content": "﻿# Topic: Basic Organization of Computer — Self-Learning\n\n**Q. Discuss the basic organization of a computer system. Explain the primary functions of the Input, Output, Memory, ALU, and Control Units.**\n\n---\n\n> 📌 **Definition to Remember**\n> The **Basic Organization of a Computer** relies on five fundamental, interconnected units that facilitate the flow of data: Input Unit, Output Unit, Memory Unit, Arithmetic and Logic Unit (ALU), and Control Unit (CU). Together, they accept, store, process, and output data.\n\n---\n\n# Basic Organization of Computer (Self-Learning)\n\n| Unit | Primary Function | Examples |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **1. Input Unit** | Accepts human-readable data/instructions, converts them into binary, and sends them to Memory. | Keyboard, Mouse, Scanner |\n| **2. Output Unit** | Retrieves binary data from Memory, translates it to human-readable format, and displays it. | Monitor, Printer, Speaker |\n| **3. Memory Unit** | Stores data and instructions. Divided into **Primary** (RAM - fast, volatile) and **Secondary** (HDD/SSD - permanent). | RAM, SSD |\n| **4. Arithmetic & Logic Unit (ALU)** | The execution engine. Performs math (add, sub) and logical (AND, OR, compare) operations. | Inside CPU |\n| **5. Control Unit (CU)** | The central nervous system. Coordinates all other units. Fetches, decodes, and issues signals to execute instructions. | Inside CPU |\n\n### 2. The Central Processing Unit (CPU)\nThe CPU is the \"brain\" of the computer. It is physically made up of:\n* **Control Unit (CU)**\n* **Arithmetic and Logic Unit (ALU)**\n* **Registers** (small, extremely fast internal memory).\n\n### 3. Basic Data Flow\n1. User types data → **Input Unit** → translates to binary.\n2. Data is stored in **Memory Unit** (RAM).\n3. **Control Unit** fetches instructions and directs data to the **ALU**.\n4. **ALU** processes the data and sends results back to Memory.\n5. **Output Unit** retrieves results from Memory and displays them to the user.\n\n---\n\n> ⭐ **Must-Write Points (for 10 marks)**\n> 1. A computer is organized into 5 units: **Input, Output, Memory, ALU, Control Unit**.\n> 2. **Input Unit**: Converts external data to binary and feeds it to the system.\n> 3. **Output Unit**: Converts binary processed data back to human-readable form.\n> 4. **Memory Unit**: Stores data/programs (Primary = RAM; Secondary = Hard Drive).\n> 5. **ALU (Arithmetic Logic Unit)**: Performs actual math and logical processing.\n> 6. **CU (Control Unit)**: Manages/coordinates all units (Fetches, Decodes, executes).\n> 7. The **CPU** is the combination of the ALU, CU, and Registers.\n\n---\n\n> ⚡ **Quick Recall**\n> `Computer Org → Input (data in) → Memory (storage) → CU (manager) → ALU (processor) → Output (data out) → CPU = CU + ALU + Registers`\n\n",
    "html": "<p>﻿# Topic: Basic Organization of Computer — Self-Learning</p>\n<p><strong>Q. Discuss the basic organization of a computer system. Explain the primary functions of the Input, Output, Memory, ALU, and Control Units.</strong></p>\n<hr />\n<blockquote>\n<p>📌 <strong>Definition to Remember</strong><br />\nThe <strong>Basic Organization of a Computer</strong> relies on five fundamental, interconnected units that facilitate the flow of data: Input Unit, Output Unit, Memory Unit, Arithmetic and Logic Unit (ALU), and Control Unit (CU). Together, they accept, store, process, and output data.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr />\n<h1>Basic Organization of Computer (Self-Learning)</h1>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Unit</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Primary Function</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Examples</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>1. Input Unit</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Accepts human-readable data/instructions, converts them into binary, and sends them to Memory.</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Keyboard, Mouse, Scanner</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>2. Output Unit</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Retrieves binary data from Memory, translates it to human-readable format, and displays it.</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Monitor, Printer, Speaker</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>3. Memory Unit</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Stores data and instructions. Divided into <strong>Primary</strong> (RAM - fast, volatile) and <strong>Secondary</strong> (HDD/SSD - permanent).</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">RAM, SSD</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>4. Arithmetic &amp; Logic Unit (ALU)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">The execution engine. Performs math (add, sub) and logical (AND, OR, compare) operations.</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Inside CPU</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>5. Control Unit (CU)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">The central nervous system. Coordinates all other units. Fetches, decodes, and issues signals to execute instructions.</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Inside CPU</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>2. The Central Processing Unit (CPU)</h3>\n<p>The CPU is the \"brain\" of the computer. It is physically made up of:<br />\n* <strong>Control Unit (CU)</strong><br />\n* <strong>Arithmetic and Logic Unit (ALU)</strong><br />\n* <strong>Registers</strong> (small, extremely fast internal memory).</p>\n<h3>3. Basic Data Flow</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>User types data → <strong>Input Unit</strong> → translates to binary.</li>\n<li>Data is stored in <strong>Memory Unit</strong> (RAM).</li>\n<li><strong>Control Unit</strong> fetches instructions and directs data to the <strong>ALU</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>ALU</strong> processes the data and sends results back to Memory.</li>\n<li><strong>Output Unit</strong> retrieves results from Memory and displays them to the user.</li>\n</ol>\n<hr />\n<blockquote>\n<p>⭐ <strong>Must-Write Points (for 10 marks)</strong><br />\n1. A computer is organized into 5 units: <strong>Input, Output, Memory, ALU, Control Unit</strong>.<br />\n2. <strong>Input Unit</strong>: Converts external data to binary and feeds it to the system.<br />\n3. <strong>Output Unit</strong>: Converts binary processed data back to human-readable form.<br />\n4. <strong>Memory Unit</strong>: Stores data/programs (Primary = RAM; Secondary = Hard Drive).<br />\n5. <strong>ALU (Arithmetic Logic Unit)</strong>: Performs actual math and logical processing.<br />\n6. <strong>CU (Control Unit)</strong>: Manages/coordinates all units (Fetches, Decodes, executes).<br />\n7. The <strong>CPU</strong> is the combination of the ALU, CU, and Registers.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr />\n<blockquote>\n<p>⚡ <strong>Quick Recall</strong><br />\n<code>Computer Org → Input (data in) → Memory (storage) → CU (manager) → ALU (processor) → Output (data out) → CPU = CU + ALU + Registers</code></p>\n</blockquote>",
