    "content": "﻿# Topic: Evolution of Computers — Self-Learning\n\n**Q. Trace the evolution of computers through their five generations. Highlight the key technological advancements and characteristics of each generation.**\n\n---\n\n> 📌 **Definition to Remember**\n> The **Evolution of Computers** is divided into five **\"Generations\"**, each marked by a major technological breakthrough that made computers smaller, cheaper, faster, and more reliable. The progression moved from Vacuum Tubes → Transistors → Integrated Circuits → Microprocessors → Artificial Intelligence.\n\n---\n\n# Evolution of Computers (Self-Learning)\n\n| Generation | Core Technology | Key Characteristics & Examples |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **First (1940s - 1950s)** | **Vacuum Tubes** (circuitry) & Magnetic Drums (memory). | Massive (room-sized), high heat, high power consumption, very unreliable. Used binary Machine Language.<br>**Ex:** ENIAC, UNIVAC. |\n| **Second (1950s - 1960s)** | **Transistors** | Smaller, faster, more energy-efficient than vacuum tubes. Transitioned to Assembly language and early high-level languages (COBOL, FORTRAN).<br>**Ex:** IBM 7094. |\n| **Third (1960s - 1970s)** | **Integrated Circuits (ICs)** | Miniaturized transistors placed on silicon chips. Introduced keyboards, monitors, and Operating Systems (multi-programming).<br>**Ex:** IBM 360 series. |\n| **Fourth (1970s - Present)** | **Microprocessors** (VLSI) | Thousands of ICs on a single silicon chip (Very Large Scale Integration). Led to personal computers, GUIs, mouse, and networking/Internet.<br>**Ex:** Apple Mac, IBM PC. |\n| **Fifth (Present & Beyond)**| **Artificial Intelligence (AI)** | Based on parallel processing and Machine Learning. Focuses on natural language processing, self-organization, and quantum computing.<br>**Ex:** Siri, Alexa, IBM Watson. |\n\n---\n\n> ⭐ **Must-Write Points (for 10 marks)**\n> 1. Computer evolution is categorized into **Five Generations**.\n> 2. **1st Gen**: Used **Vacuum tubes**; massive, generated high heat, used machine language (ENIAC).\n> 3. **2nd Gen**: Replaced tubes with **Transistors**; smaller/faster, used assembly and high-level languages (COBOL).\n> 4. **3rd Gen**: Used **Integrated Circuits (ICs)** on silicon chips; introduced OS, keyboards, monitors.\n> 5. **4th Gen**: Used **Microprocessors (VLSI)**; brought computers to desktops, introduced networking/Internet.\n> 6. **5th Gen**: Focused on **Artificial Intelligence (AI)** and parallel processing; natural language understanding.\n> 7. The consistent trend across generations: Smaller size, lower cost, higher speed, and greater reliability.\n\n---\n\n> ⚡ **Quick Recall**\n> `Generations: 1st (Vacuum Tubes, Room size) → 2nd (Transistors, Assembly Lang) → 3rd (Integrated Circuits, OS) → 4th (Microprocessors/VLSI, PCs/Internet) → 5th (AI, Machine Learning)`\n\n",
    "html": "<p>﻿# Topic: Evolution of Computers — Self-Learning</p>\n<p><strong>Q. Trace the evolution of computers through their five generations. Highlight the key technological advancements and characteristics of each generation.</strong></p>\n<hr />\n<blockquote>\n<p>📌 <strong>Definition to Remember</strong><br />\nThe <strong>Evolution of Computers</strong> is divided into five <strong>\"Generations\"</strong>, each marked by a major technological breakthrough that made computers smaller, cheaper, faster, and more reliable. The progression moved from Vacuum Tubes → Transistors → Integrated Circuits → Microprocessors → Artificial Intelligence.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr />\n<h1>Evolution of Computers (Self-Learning)</h1>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Generation</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Core Technology</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Key Characteristics &amp; Examples</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>First (1940s - 1950s)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Vacuum Tubes</strong> (circuitry) &amp; Magnetic Drums (memory).</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Massive (room-sized), high heat, high power consumption, very unreliable. Used binary Machine Language.<br><strong>Ex:</strong> ENIAC, UNIVAC.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Second (1950s - 1960s)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Transistors</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Smaller, faster, more energy-efficient than vacuum tubes. Transitioned to Assembly language and early high-level languages (COBOL, FORTRAN).<br><strong>Ex:</strong> IBM 7094.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Third (1960s - 1970s)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Integrated Circuits (ICs)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Miniaturized transistors placed on silicon chips. Introduced keyboards, monitors, and Operating Systems (multi-programming).<br><strong>Ex:</strong> IBM 360 series.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Fourth (1970s - Present)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Microprocessors</strong> (VLSI)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Thousands of ICs on a single silicon chip (Very Large Scale Integration). Led to personal computers, GUIs, mouse, and networking/Internet.<br><strong>Ex:</strong> Apple Mac, IBM PC.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Fifth (Present &amp; Beyond)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Artificial Intelligence (AI)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Based on parallel processing and Machine Learning. Focuses on natural language processing, self-organization, and quantum computing.<br><strong>Ex:</strong> Siri, Alexa, IBM Watson.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<hr />\n<blockquote>\n<p>⭐ <strong>Must-Write Points (for 10 marks)</strong><br />\n1. Computer evolution is categorized into <strong>Five Generations</strong>.<br />\n2. <strong>1st Gen</strong>: Used <strong>Vacuum tubes</strong>; massive, generated high heat, used machine language (ENIAC).<br />\n3. <strong>2nd Gen</strong>: Replaced tubes with <strong>Transistors</strong>; smaller/faster, used assembly and high-level languages (COBOL).<br />\n4. <strong>3rd Gen</strong>: Used <strong>Integrated Circuits (ICs)</strong> on silicon chips; introduced OS, keyboards, monitors.<br />\n5. <strong>4th Gen</strong>: Used <strong>Microprocessors (VLSI)</strong>; brought computers to desktops, introduced networking/Internet.<br />\n6. <strong>5th Gen</strong>: Focused on <strong>Artificial Intelligence (AI)</strong> and parallel processing; natural language understanding.<br />\n7. The consistent trend across generations: Smaller size, lower cost, higher speed, and greater reliability.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr />\n<blockquote>\n<p>⚡ <strong>Quick Recall</strong><br />\n<code>Generations: 1st (Vacuum Tubes, Room size) → 2nd (Transistors, Assembly Lang) → 3rd (Integrated Circuits, OS) → 4th (Microprocessors/VLSI, PCs/Internet) → 5th (AI, Machine Learning)</code></p>\n</blockquote>",
