    "title": "Module 2: 2-Mark Questions & Answers (CPU & Pipelining)",
    "content": "# 2-Mark Questions & Answers — MPCA Unit 2: Processor Organization & Architecture\n\n---\n\n### Q1. Define pipelining.\n\n**Pipelining** is an instruction execution technique where multiple instructions are overlapped in execution across independent hardware stages (Fetch, Decode, Execute, Memory, Write-back), increasing instruction throughput without increasing individual execution time.\n\n---\n\n### Q2. What is a pipeline hazard?\n\nA **Pipeline Hazard** is any condition or situation (such as structural resource contention, data dependencies, or branch instructions) that prevents the next instruction in the pipeline stream from executing in its designated clock cycle.\n\n---\n\n### Q3. List Flynn's classification of computer architecture.\n\n1. **SISD:** Single Instruction Stream, Single Data Stream.\n2. **SIMD:** Single Instruction Stream, Multiple Data Streams.\n3. **MISD:** Multiple Instruction Streams, Single Data Stream.\n4. **MIMD:** Multiple Instruction Streams, Multiple Data Streams.\n\n---\n\n### Q4. What is microprogrammed control unit?\n\nA **Microprogrammed Control Unit** is a control unit design where control signals are generated by reading microinstructions (stored in a Control Memory ROM) sequentially, rather than using fixed hardwired logic gates.\n\n---\n\n### Q5. Define instruction cycle.\n\nAn **Instruction Cycle** (or Fetch-Decode-Execute cycle) is the sequence of operations performed by the CPU to execute a single machine instruction. It consists of the **Fetch phase**, **Decode phase**, **Effective Address calculation phase**, **Execute phase**, and **Interrupt service check phase**.\n",
    "html": "<h1>2-Mark Questions &amp; Answers — MPCA Unit 2: Processor Organization &amp; Architecture</h1>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q1. Define pipelining.</h3>\n<p><strong>Pipelining</strong> is an instruction execution technique where multiple instructions are overlapped in execution across independent hardware stages (Fetch, Decode, Execute, Memory, Write-back), increasing instruction throughput without increasing individual execution time.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q2. What is a pipeline hazard?</h3>\n<p>A <strong>Pipeline Hazard</strong> is any condition or situation (such as structural resource contention, data dependencies, or branch instructions) that prevents the next instruction in the pipeline stream from executing in its designated clock cycle.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q3. List Flynn's classification of computer architecture.</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>SISD:</strong> Single Instruction Stream, Single Data Stream.</li>\n<li><strong>SIMD:</strong> Single Instruction Stream, Multiple Data Streams.</li>\n<li><strong>MISD:</strong> Multiple Instruction Streams, Single Data Stream.</li>\n<li><strong>MIMD:</strong> Multiple Instruction Streams, Multiple Data Streams.</li>\n</ol>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q4. What is microprogrammed control unit?</h3>\n<p>A <strong>Microprogrammed Control Unit</strong> is a control unit design where control signals are generated by reading microinstructions (stored in a Control Memory ROM) sequentially, rather than using fixed hardwired logic gates.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q5. Define instruction cycle.</h3>\n<p>An <strong>Instruction Cycle</strong> (or Fetch-Decode-Execute cycle) is the sequence of operations performed by the CPU to execute a single machine instruction. It consists of the <strong>Fetch phase</strong>, <strong>Decode phase</strong>, <strong>Effective Address calculation phase</strong>, <strong>Execute phase</strong>, and <strong>Interrupt service check phase</strong>.</p>",
