    "title": "Module 3: 2-Mark Questions & Answers (8086 Microprocessor)",
    "content": "# 2-Mark Questions & Answers — MPCA Unit 3: The Intel Microprocessor 8086 Architecture\n\n---\n\n### Q1. List the functional units of the 8086 microprocessor.\n\nThe 8086 CPU is divided into two independent functional units:\n1. **Bus Interface Unit (BIU):** Manages instruction prefetching, memory bus communication, segment registers, and physical address generation.\n2. **Execution Unit (EU):** Decodes micro-instructions, executes arithmetic/logical operations in ALU, and manages general-purpose registers and flags.\n\n---\n\n### Q2. What is memory segmentation in 8086?\n\n**Memory Segmentation** is a technique that divides the 1MB physical memory space into 64KB logical blocks called segments (Code Segment, Data Segment, Stack Segment, Extra Segment). It allows a 16-bit processor to address 20-bit physical addresses using segment base registers and offset registers.\n\n---\n\n### Q3. Define minimum mode and maximum mode of 8086.\n\n- **Minimum Mode (MN/MX' = 1):** Single-processor system mode where 8086 generates all bus control signals (`RD`, `WR`, `ALE`, `M/IO`) directly.\n- **Maximum Mode (MN/MX' = 0):** Multi-processor system mode where bus control signals are generated by an external Bus Controller chip (Intel 8288).\n\n---\n\n### Q4. What is the purpose of demultiplexing the address/data bus?\n\nThe 8086 uses multiplexed pins **AD0 - AD15** to save physical pin count. **Demultiplexing** separates the 20-bit memory address from 16-bit data using octal latches (74LS373) driven by the Address Latch Enable (`ALE`) signal.\n\n---\n\n### Q5. State the size of the 8086 instruction queue.\n\nThe 8086 Bus Interface Unit (BIU) contains a **6-byte Instruction Queue** that prefetches instruction bytes sequentially from memory when the EU is busy, enabling instruction pipelined prefetching.\n",
    "html": "<h1>2-Mark Questions &amp; Answers — MPCA Unit 3: The Intel Microprocessor 8086 Architecture</h1>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q1. List the functional units of the 8086 microprocessor.</h3>\n<p>The 8086 CPU is divided into two independent functional units:<br />\n1. <strong>Bus Interface Unit (BIU):</strong> Manages instruction prefetching, memory bus communication, segment registers, and physical address generation.<br />\n2. <strong>Execution Unit (EU):</strong> Decodes micro-instructions, executes arithmetic/logical operations in ALU, and manages general-purpose registers and flags.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q2. What is memory segmentation in 8086?</h3>\n<p><strong>Memory Segmentation</strong> is a technique that divides the 1MB physical memory space into 64KB logical blocks called segments (Code Segment, Data Segment, Stack Segment, Extra Segment). It allows a 16-bit processor to address 20-bit physical addresses using segment base registers and offset registers.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q3. Define minimum mode and maximum mode of 8086.</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Minimum Mode (MN/MX' = 1):</strong> Single-processor system mode where 8086 generates all bus control signals (<code>RD</code>, <code>WR</code>, <code>ALE</code>, <code>M/IO</code>) directly.</li>\n<li><strong>Maximum Mode (MN/MX' = 0):</strong> Multi-processor system mode where bus control signals are generated by an external Bus Controller chip (Intel 8288).</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q4. What is the purpose of demultiplexing the address/data bus?</h3>\n<p>The 8086 uses multiplexed pins <strong>AD0 - AD15</strong> to save physical pin count. <strong>Demultiplexing</strong> separates the 20-bit memory address from 16-bit data using octal latches (74LS373) driven by the Address Latch Enable (<code>ALE</code>) signal.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q5. State the size of the 8086 instruction queue.</h3>\n<p>The 8086 Bus Interface Unit (BIU) contains a <strong>6-byte Instruction Queue</strong> that prefetches instruction bytes sequentially from memory when the EU is busy, enabling instruction pipelined prefetching.</p>",
    "definition": "",
