    "title": "Module 3: 3-Mark Questions & Answers (8086 Microprocessor)",
    "content": "# 3-Mark Questions & Answers — MPCA Unit 3: The Intel Microprocessor 8086 Architecture\n\n---\n\n### Q1. Draw and explain the functional pin diagram of 8086 (any 6-8 important pins).\n\n1. **AD0 - AD15 (Pins 2-16, 39):** Multiplexed Address/Data lines carrying 16-bit data or lower 16-bit memory address.\n2. **A16/S3 - A19/S6 (Pins 35-38):** Multiplexed upper 4-bit address lines and status signals.\n3. **ALE (Address Latch Enable):** High pulse indicating valid address is present on multiplexed bus.\n4. **RD / WR:** Memory/IO Read and Write control output signals.\n5. **MN/MX' (Pin 33):** Configures CPU for Minimum Mode (+5V) or Maximum Mode (GND).\n6. **RESET (Pin 21):** Resets CPU; sets `CS = FFFFH`, `IP = 0000H`.\n\n---\n\n### Q2. Explain memory segmentation in 8086 with a diagram.\n\n```\n1 MB Memory Space (00000H to FFFFFH)\n+------------------------------------+ FFFFFH\n|      UNMAPPED MEMORY SPACE         |\n+------------------------------------+\n|  EXTRA SEGMENT (ES)     (64 KB)    | <- ES:DI\n+------------------------------------+\n|  STACK SEGMENT (SS)     (64 KB)    | <- SS:SP/BP\n+------------------------------------+\n|  DATA SEGMENT (DS)      (64 KB)    | <- DS:BX/SI\n+------------------------------------+\n|  CODE SEGMENT (CS)      (64 KB)    | <- CS:IP\n+------------------------------------+ 00000H\n```\n- Total Physical Address Space = `2^20 = 1 MB`.\n- Divided into 4 active 64KB logical segments: **CS** (Code), **DS** (Data), **SS** (Stack), **ES** (Extra).\n\n---\n\n### Q3. Explain the concept of banking (memory banking) in 8086.\n\n```\n         1MB PHYSICAL MEMORY\n       /                       EVEN BANK (Even Bytes)     ODD BANK (Odd Bytes)\n  512 KB (D0 - D7)           512 KB (D8 - D15)\n  Connected to A0 = 0        Connected to BHE' = 0\n```\n- Memory is split into two 512KB banks:\n  - **Even Bank (Lower Bank):** Contains even byte addresses (`00000H, 00002H, ...`), driven by `A0 = 0`.\n  - **Odd Bank (Upper Bank):** Contains odd byte addresses (`00001H, 00003H, ...`), driven by `BHE' = 0`.\n- Enables fetching 16-bit word data in a single memory cycle when aligned on an even address.\n\n---\n\n### Q4. Differentiate between minimum mode and maximum mode operation of 8086.\n\n| Feature | Minimum Mode | Maximum Mode |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Pin Setup** | Pin 33 (MN/MX') connected to +5V. | Pin 33 (MN/MX') connected to GND. |\n| **System Architecture**| Single CPU processor system. | Multi-processor / Co-processor system (8087 FPU, 8089 IOP). |\n| **Control Generator** | 8086 directly outputs `RD`, `WR`, `ALE`, `M/IO`. | External 8288 Bus Controller decodes status pins `S0', S1', S2'`. |\n\n---\n\n### Q5. Explain the need for demultiplexing the address/data bus in 8086.\n\n1. Pins **AD0 - AD15** carry memory addresses during the first clock cycle (`T1`) and data during `T2, T3, T4`.\n2. Without demultiplexing, memory chips would lose the target address when data values are placed on the pins during `T2`.\n3. Latches (74LS373) latch the address when `ALE = 1`, preserving a stable 20-bit address throughout the memory cycle.\n",
    "html": "<h1>3-Mark Questions &amp; Answers — MPCA Unit 3: The Intel Microprocessor 8086 Architecture</h1>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q1. Draw and explain the functional pin diagram of 8086 (any 6-8 important pins).</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>AD0 - AD15 (Pins 2-16, 39):</strong> Multiplexed Address/Data lines carrying 16-bit data or lower 16-bit memory address.</li>\n<li><strong>A16/S3 - A19/S6 (Pins 35-38):</strong> Multiplexed upper 4-bit address lines and status signals.</li>\n<li><strong>ALE (Address Latch Enable):</strong> High pulse indicating valid address is present on multiplexed bus.</li>\n<li><strong>RD / WR:</strong> Memory/IO Read and Write control output signals.</li>\n<li><strong>MN/MX' (Pin 33):</strong> Configures CPU for Minimum Mode (+5V) or Maximum Mode (GND).</li>\n<li><strong>RESET (Pin 21):</strong> Resets CPU; sets <code>CS = FFFFH</code>, <code>IP = 0000H</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q2. Explain memory segmentation in 8086 with a diagram.</h3>\n<pre><code>1 MB Memory Space (00000H to FFFFFH)\n+------------------------------------+ FFFFFH\n|      UNMAPPED MEMORY SPACE         |\n+------------------------------------+\n|  EXTRA SEGMENT (ES)     (64 KB)    | &lt;- ES:DI\n+------------------------------------+\n|  STACK SEGMENT (SS)     (64 KB)    | &lt;- SS:SP/BP\n+------------------------------------+\n|  DATA SEGMENT (DS)      (64 KB)    | &lt;- DS:BX/SI\n+------------------------------------+\n|  CODE SEGMENT (CS)      (64 KB)    | &lt;- CS:IP\n+------------------------------------+ 00000H\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>Total Physical Address Space = <code>2^20 = 1 MB</code>.</li>\n<li>Divided into 4 active 64KB logical segments: <strong>CS</strong> (Code), <strong>DS</strong> (Data), <strong>SS</strong> (Stack), <strong>ES</strong> (Extra).</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q3. Explain the concept of banking (memory banking) in 8086.</h3>\n<pre><code>         1MB PHYSICAL MEMORY\n       /                       EVEN BANK (Even Bytes)     ODD BANK (Odd Bytes)\n  512 KB (D0 - D7)           512 KB (D8 - D15)\n  Connected to A0 = 0        Connected to BHE' = 0\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>Memory is split into two 512KB banks:</li>\n<li><strong>Even Bank (Lower Bank):</strong> Contains even byte addresses (<code>00000H, 00002H, ...</code>), driven by <code>A0 = 0</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Odd Bank (Upper Bank):</strong> Contains odd byte addresses (<code>00001H, 00003H, ...</code>), driven by <code>BHE' = 0</code>.</li>\n<li>Enables fetching 16-bit word data in a single memory cycle when aligned on an even address.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q4. Differentiate between minimum mode and maximum mode operation of 8086.</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Feature</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Minimum Mode</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Maximum Mode</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Pin Setup</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Pin 33 (MN/MX') connected to +5V.</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Pin 33 (MN/MX') connected to GND.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>System Architecture</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Single CPU processor system.</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Multi-processor / Co-processor system (8087 FPU, 8089 IOP).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Control Generator</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">8086 directly outputs <code>RD</code>, <code>WR</code>, <code>ALE</code>, <code>M/IO</code>.</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">External 8288 Bus Controller decodes status pins <code>S0', S1', S2'</code>.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q5. Explain the need for demultiplexing the address/data bus in 8086.</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Pins <strong>AD0 - AD15</strong> carry memory addresses during the first clock cycle (<code>T1</code>) and data during <code>T2, T3, T4</code>.</li>\n<li>Without demultiplexing, memory chips would lose the target address when data values are placed on the pins during <code>T2</code>.</li>\n<li>Latches (74LS373) latch the address when <code>ALE = 1</code>, preserving a stable 20-bit address throughout the memory cycle.</li>\n</ol>",
