    "title": "Module 5: 3-Mark Questions & Answers (80386DX & Pentium Processor)",
    "content": "# 3-Mark Questions & Answers — MPCA Module 5: 80386DX & Pentium Processors\n\n---\n\n### Q1. Explain the 4 privilege rings of 80386 Protected Mode.\n\n- **Ring 0 (Kernel Core):** Highest privilege; executes privileged OS instructions.\n- **Ring 1 (Device Drivers):** System services and drivers.\n- **Ring 2 (OS Extensions):** Middleware and database engines.\n- **Ring 3 (User Applications):** Lowest privilege; untrusted user applications.\n\n---\n\n### Q2. Explain the 5 pipeline stages of Pentium processor.\n\n1. **Prefetch (PF):** Fetches instructions from 8 KB Code Cache.\n2. **Decode-1 (D1):** Decodes opcode and determines U/V pairability.\n3. **Decode-2 (D2):** Generates memory addresses for operands.\n4. **Execute (EX):** ALU integer execution and Data Cache access.\n5. **Writeback (WB):** Updates destination registers and flags.\n\n---\n\n### Q3. Explain two-level Paging in 80386 microprocessor.\n\nLinear address (32-bit) is divided into:\n- `Bits 31-22 (10 bits)`: Page Directory Index.\n- `Bits 21-12 (10 bits)`: Page Table Index.\n- `Bits 11-0 (12 bits)`: Offset within 4 KB Page Frame.\n- `CR3` register holds the physical base address of the Page Directory.\n",
    "html": "<h1>3-Mark Questions &amp; Answers — MPCA Module 5: 80386DX &amp; Pentium Processors</h1>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q1. Explain the 4 privilege rings of 80386 Protected Mode.</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ring 0 (Kernel Core):</strong> Highest privilege; executes privileged OS instructions.</li>\n<li><strong>Ring 1 (Device Drivers):</strong> System services and drivers.</li>\n<li><strong>Ring 2 (OS Extensions):</strong> Middleware and database engines.</li>\n<li><strong>Ring 3 (User Applications):</strong> Lowest privilege; untrusted user applications.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q2. Explain the 5 pipeline stages of Pentium processor.</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Prefetch (PF):</strong> Fetches instructions from 8 KB Code Cache.</li>\n<li><strong>Decode-1 (D1):</strong> Decodes opcode and determines U/V pairability.</li>\n<li><strong>Decode-2 (D2):</strong> Generates memory addresses for operands.</li>\n<li><strong>Execute (EX):</strong> ALU integer execution and Data Cache access.</li>\n<li><strong>Writeback (WB):</strong> Updates destination registers and flags.</li>\n</ol>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q3. Explain two-level Paging in 80386 microprocessor.</h3>\n<p>Linear address (32-bit) is divided into:<br />\n- <code>Bits 31-22 (10 bits)</code>: Page Directory Index.<br />\n- <code>Bits 21-12 (10 bits)</code>: Page Table Index.<br />\n- <code>Bits 11-0 (12 bits)</code>: Offset within 4 KB Page Frame.<br />\n- <code>CR3</code> register holds the physical base address of the Page Directory.</p>",
